# DuDuClaw OS — A/B GPT layout (Y8-1, 2026-08-27).
#
# Yocto port of the Debian appliance line's mkosi.repart/ four-partition
# design (commercial/docs/DESIGN-ab-update-rollback-2026-08.md §1.1, §11):
# ESP + root-A (populated, active) + root-B (empty, reserved update target)
# + /data (grows to fill the real disk, hosts update staging). The MECHANISM
# is ported verbatim (same partition roles, same GPT type GUID, same
# version/`_empty` label scheme, same three attribute bits); the FORM is
# Yocto-native because wic's kickstart-style partitioning language is a
# different tool with a smaller vocabulary than mkosi.repart's declarative
# `.conf` drop-ins — see meta-duduclaw/classes/duduclaw-ab-partflags.bbclass
# for the one thing wic cannot express in-line (raw GPT attribute bits) and
# how this build fills that gap.
#
# FIELD-MAPPING CORRECTION (Y9-2, 2026-08-27): mkosi.repart's `Label=`
# directive writes the GPT partition Name field directly; wic's kickstart
# `--label` option does NOT — it only sets the ext4 filesystem label
# (mkfs -L), a field invisible to systemd-sysupdate's raw-GPT
# `[Target] MatchPattern=` matching and to appliance/tools/uki-slots.py's
# `read_gpt()`. The first real `make-payload.py` run against a build of
# this file (before this correction) reproduced the Debian line's own
# already-fixed-once H3a bug byte-for-byte (root-A's on-disk GPT name read
# back as the static role string `duduclaw-root-a`, never a version) —
# see p2/p3's own comments below for the full evidence trail. wic's
# `--part-name` is the option that actually reaches the GPT Name field;
# p2/p3 below carry the version/`_empty` scheme there now, with `--label`
# repurposed as a purely cosmetic ext4 label.
#
# p1 (ESP) is copied byte-for-byte from the already-QEMU-verified single-
# partition efi-uki-bootdisk.wks.in (openembedded-core/meta/files/wic/) —
# deliberately unchanged, so the UKI/systemd-boot discovery chain that
# passed Y1-1 stays exactly as it was. Everything from p2 onward is new.
#
# WHY A SEPARATE FILE, NOT AN EDIT TO efi-uki-bootdisk.wks.in: this is a
# structurally different, higher-risk partition table (four partitions
# instead of one, GPT attribute post-processing required — see the
# bbclass). Per this project's own established pattern for risky additions
# on top of a verified baseline (duduclaw-image.bb `require`s
# duduclaw-image-minimal.bb rather than editing it in place;
# duduclaw-image-flatpak.bb does the same to duduclaw-image.bb), the A/B
# layout is opted into by a NEW image recipe
# (recipes-core/images/duduclaw-image-ab.bb) that overrides WKS_FILE to
# point here, rather than mutating the wks every existing image recipe
# already depends on and has QEMU evidence for.
#
# SIZES: `DUDUCLAW_AB_SLOT_SIZE_MB` / `DUDUCLAW_AB_DATA_SIZE_MB` are plain
# bitbake variables (defaults set in duduclaw-ab-partflags.bbclass,
# overridable per image recipe) — `.wks.in` files go through bitbake's own
# `${VAR}` expansion before wic ever sees them
# (image_types_wic.bbclass:do_write_wks_template, confirmed by reading that
# class, not assumed from the `.in` suffix convention alone). Root-A and
# root-B MUST stay the same size: systemd-sysupdate dd's a slot-A-sized
# payload into slot B with no resize step (same "required for
# systemd-sysupdate" invariant the Debian line's own 20-root-a.conf/
# 21-root-b.conf comments state).
#
# ARCHITECTURE: this file is written for x86_64 only (the
# duduclaw-genericx86-64 / duduclaw-qemux86-64 machines — this layer has no
# arm64 target). The GPT type GUID below
# (4F68BCE3-E8CD-4DB1-96E7-FBCAF984B709) is systemd's
# SD_GPT_ROOT_X86_64 — verified against the actual pinned systemd source
# (src/systemd/sd-gpt.h at SRCREV b3d8fc43e9cb531d958c17ef2cd93b374bc14e8a),
# not copied from the Debian design doc's own arm64-only build without
# re-checking — the Debian design doc's §1.5 forensic dump was against an
# arm64 mkosi artifact (`root-arm64`, GUID b921b045-...) and is NOT the
# right constant to reuse here.

bootloader --ptable gpt --timeout=5

# p1: ESP — same as efi-uki-bootdisk.wks.in EXCEPT `--fixed-size
# ${DUDUCLAW_AB_ESP_SIZE_MB}` (Y9-2, 2026-08-27): the single-partition
# base template lets wic auto-size the ESP off whatever it happens to
# contain at build time (one UKI + rescue), which is provably too small
# once a second (incoming update) UKI needs to coexist with it — see
# classes/duduclaw-ab-partflags.bbclass's own comment on
# DUDUCLAW_AB_ESP_SIZE_MB for the exact reproduction (a real
# `device.update_apply` run hit ENOSPC writing the new UKI). `--fixed-size`
# combines with `--source` the same way p2/p3 below already rely on
# (content populates it, --fixed-size overrides the total partition size)
# — EXCEPT `--overhead-factor` had to be dropped entirely, not just left
# at its old value: wic's ksparser rejects `--overhead-factor` (and
# `--extra-filesystem-space`) as mutually exclusive with `--fixed-size`
# ("arguments --overhead-factor and --extra-filesystem-space not allowed
# with argument --fixed-size" — confirmed by an actual failed
# `do_image_wic` run during this ticket, not assumed). That is fine here:
# `--overhead-factor` only mattered for wic's OWN auto-sizing math, which
# `--fixed-size` replaces outright.
part /boot --source bootimg_efi --sourceparams="loader=${EFI_PROVIDER}" --label boot --active --align 1024 --use-uuid --uuid="${DUDUCLAW_AB_ESP_PARTUUID}" --part-name="ESP" --part-type=C12A7328-F81F-11D2-BA4B-00A0C93EC93B --fixed-size ${DUDUCLAW_AB_ESP_SIZE_MB}

# p2: root-A — the only partition with real content at build time.
# `--part-name` (NOT `--label`) is the field that actually lands in the raw
# GPT partition entry's Name field — the exact 72-byte region
# appliance/tools/uki-slots.py's `read_gpt()` decodes as `.label`
# (`raw_entry[56:128].decode("utf-16-le")`), and the same field
# systemd-sysupdate's own `[Target] MatchPattern=` in
# recipes-duduclaw/duduclaw-ab-update/files/10-duduclaw-root.transfer
# matches against ("Matched against the partition LABEL" — systemd's own
# terminology for this GPT field, not wic's `--label` kickstart option,
# which instead only sets the ext4 filesystem label via mkfs -L and is
# invisible to both sysupdate and any tool doing a raw GPT parse). This is
# the SAME distinction the Debian appliance line's own
# appliance/mkosi.repart/20-root-a.conf already documents having gotten
# wrong once and fixed (H3a, 2026-08-23: "the old `duduclaw-root-a` matched
# no pattern, so `systemd-sysupdate list` reported ZERO installed
# instances") — mkosi.repart's `Label=` directive maps onto the GPT
# partition Name field directly, but wic's `--label` kickstart option does
# NOT (`--part-name` does); a first real build+`make-payload.py` run
# against this line's own .wic (Y9-2, 2026-08-27) reproduced the identical
# failure via `read_gpt()` reporting the on-disk name as `duduclaw-root-a`
# instead of the intended version string, confirming this was a real
# regression of the already-fixed Debian-line bug, not a new distinct
# issue. `${DUDUCLAW_PLATFORM_VERSION}` is the same single-source-of-truth
# version this layer already uses for DISTRO_VERSION
# (conf/distro/include/duduclaw-platform-version.inc) — using it here means
# a factory image's own root-A GPT name always matches what os-release's
# `IMAGE_VERSION=` reports, which
# `crates/duduclaw-gateway/src/os_update.rs`'s `ProtectVersion=%A` depends
# on to protect the running slot from ever being treated as a free target.
# `--label` is kept as a purely cosmetic ext4 filesystem label (never read
# by sysupdate, make-payload.py, or gpt-auto — this line's root mount is by
# `root=PARTUUID=` baked into the UKI, not by fstab LABEL=/UUID=, and p2 has
# neither `--use-uuid` nor `--use-label` so wic never emits an fstab line
# for it either) so `e2label`/`lsblk -o LABEL` still show something
# human-readable.
#
# WS-3/A6 (2026-09-01, DESIGN-os-security-line-2026-09.md §2 支柱一 A6):
# `${DISTRO_VERSION}` here, NOT the bare `${DUDUCLAW_PLATFORM_VERSION}` this
# line used from Y9-2 through the WS-3 wave. Root cause this closes (fully
# documented in recipes-duduclaw/duduclaw-ab-update/files/
# 10-duduclaw-root.transfer's own header, flagged there as an explicit Y8-1
# followup, not silently carried): `[Transfer] ProtectVersion=%A` in that
# same file compares against `%A`, systemd-sysupdate's specifier for the
# RUNNING image's `IMAGE_VERSION=` from /usr/lib/os-release — and oe-core's
# own os-release.bbclass sets `IMAGE_VERSION = "${DISTRO_VERSION}"`
# (verified against the pinned openembedded-core source, not assumed), i.e.
# the FULL `${DUDUCLAW_PLATFORM_VERSION}-y1-bringup` string
# (conf/distro/duduclaw-os.conf), not the bare platform number this wks line
# used to bake into the GPT name. The two strings never matched, silently
# defeating `ProtectVersion=%A`'s entire purpose (protecting the running
# slot from ever being treated as sysupdate's own free update target).
# Fixed at the root by baking the SAME `${DISTRO_VERSION}` os-release
# already uses, rather than patching the comparison on the sysupdate side —
# this makes the fix self-consistent across time for free: whatever
# DISTRO_VERSION happens to be at ANY future build (with or without a
# milestone suffix, once this line leaves bring-up) is, by construction,
# always what THAT SAME build's own os-release reports too, so
# ProtectVersion=%A keeps working without needing to be revisited again
# when the "-y1-bringup" suffix is eventually dropped.
#
# Length checked, not assumed safe: GPT's own partition-name field is
# 36 UTF-16 code units (systemd's own `#define GPT_LABEL_MAX 36`,
# src/shared/gpt.h, read directly at this line's pinned SRCREV before
# making this change) — "duduclaw-os_1.62.0-y1-bringup" is 29 characters,
# comfortably under the limit even with the milestone suffix attached; no
# fallback "strip the suffix instead" scheme (the design doc's own stated
# alternative, for if the length/charset budget did not allow the full
# string) was needed.
#
# --uuid="${DUDUCLAW_AB_ROOTA_PARTUUID}" (Y9-2, 2026-08-27): fixes root-A's
# actual GPT PARTUUID to a build-time-known constant instead of wic's
# default `uuid.uuid4()` random value — see
# classes/duduclaw-ab-partflags.bbclass's own comment on
# DUDUCLAW_AB_ROOTA_PARTUUID for the full reasoning (do_uki runs before
# do_image_wic, so a random PARTUUID cannot be read back and baked into
# the UKI at build time the way mkosi's own `root=PARTUUID` bare-token
# substitution does). recipes-core/images/duduclaw-image-ab.bb's
# UKI_CMDLINE override bakes `root=PARTUUID=${DUDUCLAW_AB_ROOTA_PARTUUID}`
# — the SAME constant — directly into the signed UKI, so this line is
# root-A's boot-selection input for real (not the GPT name/label scheme
# above, which is sysupdate's version ledger, not a boot-time mechanism —
# see p2's own comment two paragraphs up and DESIGN-ab-update-rollback-
# 2026-08.md §4.2's "Not a boot-selection input any more" finding on the
# Debian line, which applies identically here).
#
# --source ${DUDUCLAW_AB_ROOTA_WIC_SOURCE} (VER-V, 2026-09-02): factored
# out of a hard-coded `--source rootfs --exclude-path boot/` into a
# variable — classes/duduclaw-ab-partflags.bbclass's own default for
# DUDUCLAW_AB_ROOTA_WIC_SOURCE is that exact literal string, so a build
# that never sets DUDUCLAW_VERITY_ENABLE gets a byte-identical expanded
# wks to before this change. When DUDUCLAW_VERITY_ENABLE=1,
# classes/duduclaw-verity.bbclass overrides this variable to
# `rawcopy --sourceparams="file=<ext4 image>"`, where `<ext4 image>` is a
# COPY of oe-core's own `do_image_ext4` output (IMAGE_FSTYPES:append=
# " ext4" in that class) — root-A's content then comes from a file that
# class ALSO feeds into `veritysetup format` at build time, so what gets
# hashed and what wic actually writes into this partition are the exact
# same bytes by construction (a literal file copy + a plain read, never a
# second independent `mkfs.ext4` invocation that could produce different
# bytes for the same logical content — see that class's own header,
# "REFACTORED" section, for why the original design — a hand-rolled
# `mkfs.ext4 -d` under `fakeroot` — was abandoned after real bake failures
# and replaced with reusing oe-core's own already-correct mechanism).
#
# ACCEPTED TRADE-OFF (classes/duduclaw-verity.bbclass's own header has the
# full writeup): `do_image_ext4` has no `--exclude-path boot/` equivalent,
# so when DUDUCLAW_VERITY_ENABLE=1 this partition's content — UNLIKE the
# plain `rootfs`-sourced default above — carries whatever
# `${IMAGE_ROOTFS}/boot` holds (kernel image, initramfs, etc.) instead of
# an empty placeholder directory. Harmless: /boot is never read from
# root-A at runtime (the ESP, a separate GPT partition, is what
# systemd-boot/the UKI actually boot from), only wasted space, well
# inside this slot's own multi-GB budget (DUDUCLAW_AB_SLOT_SIZE_MB).
part / --source ${DUDUCLAW_AB_ROOTA_WIC_SOURCE} --fstype=ext4 ${DUDUCLAW_AB_ROOTA_LABEL_OPT} --align 1024 --uuid="${DUDUCLAW_AB_ROOTA_PARTUUID}" --part-name="duduclaw-os_${DISTRO_VERSION}" --part-type=4F68BCE3-E8CD-4DB1-96E7-FBCAF984B709 --fixed-size ${DUDUCLAW_AB_SLOT_SIZE_MB}

# p3 (VER-V, 2026-09-02): root-a-verity — the hash tree covering root-A's
# own content, only present when DUDUCLAW_VERITY_ENABLE=1. Blank line
# (wic tolerates blank lines in a kickstart file; no partition number gets
# consumed) when the variable is unset — see
# classes/duduclaw-ab-partflags.bbclass's own comment on
# DUDUCLAW_AB_ROOTA_VERITY_WKS_LINE and
# classes/duduclaw-verity.bbclass's header for the full contract, including
# why the whole `part ...` line is built as one Python-formatted string
# rather than composed from several nested `${VAR}` references the way
# every other partition line in this file is.
${DUDUCLAW_AB_ROOTA_VERITY_WKS_LINE}

# root-B — the reserved, empty update target. Partition number 3 when
# DUDUCLAW_VERITY_ENABLE is unset (unchanged from before VER-V), 4 when
# it is set to "1" (root-a-verity, immediately above, becomes the new
# partition 3 — see classes/duduclaw-ab-partflags.bbclass's own comment on
# DUDUCLAW_AB_ROOTB_PARTNUM for the full renumbering contract; this
# comment block itself, and every partition-NUMBER reference inside it
# below, predates VER-V and is being kept accurate rather than rewritten
# wholesale). `_empty` is the ONE
# string systemd-sysupdate's `Type=partition` target recognises as "free to
# write into" (sysupdate.d(5) — this is not a DuDuClaw convention, it is the
# literal string upstream's own partition-target code matches on), and it
# MUST land in `--part-name` (the raw GPT partition Name field), not
# `--label` (ext4 filesystem label, invisible to sysupdate and to
# make-payload.py's raw GPT parse) — see p2's comment above for the full
# make-payload.py/sysupdate.d field-mapping evidence and the matching
# already-fixed-once Debian-line bug this mirrors
# (appliance/mkosi.repart/21-root-b.conf: "The old `duduclaw-root-b` did the
# opposite — it sorted ABOVE slot A's `duduclaw-root-a`, making the empty
# slot gpt-auto's preferred root"). Formatted ext4 here (unlike the Debian
# line's raw unformatted slot) purely because wic's kickstart partitioning
# requires SOME --fstype for size/empty-partition handling — this
# filesystem is irrelevant and gets completely overwritten the moment
# sysupdate (or, in a manual test, `dd`) writes a real root image into this
# partition; it carries no content that matters. `--label "root-b"` is the
# same purely-cosmetic ext4 label p2 sets, for the same reason.
# --no-fstab-update: this slot is never mounted at boot (no mountpoint
# argument), so it must never get an /etc/fstab line — an unmountable UUID
# left in fstab would fail the whole mount unit chain on generation.
# GPT attribute bits (NoAuto + ReadOnly, matching the Debian line's
# 21-root-b.conf `NoAuto=yes`/`ReadOnly=yes` factory state) are NOT settable
# in wic's kickstart syntax at all (confirmed: `ksparser.py`'s `part`
# argument list has no equivalent of mkosi.repart's `Flags=`/`NoAuto=`/
# `ReadOnly=`/`GrowFileSystem=`) — set as a post-`do_image_wic` step by
# duduclaw-ab-partflags.bbclass instead, KEYED BY PARTITION NUMBER
# (${DUDUCLAW_AB_ROOTB_PARTNUM} — 3 or 4, see this block's own opening
# paragraph above), not by name or label, so this field swap does not affect it (confirmed by
# reading that bbclass — it never references either string). Until that
# post-processing step runs, this partition is indistinguishable from an
# ordinary auto-discoverable root partition, which would make
# gpt-auto-generator's tie-break (`strverscmp_improved` picks the higher
# GPT name) the ONLY thing preventing it from being mounted — correct by
# luck (`_empty` sorts below any real version string, per Debian's own
# §4.1 finding: `_` = 0x5F < `d` = 0x64), but the NoAuto bit is what makes it
# correct on purpose, matching the design doc's own stated defense-in-depth
# reasoning for why NoAuto exists at all (§1.1: "gpt-auto-generator 看到兩個
# 候選 root 會歧義" was a REAL incident on the Debian line before that bit was
# added).
#
# --uuid="${DUDUCLAW_AB_ROOTB_PARTUUID}" (T4, 2026-09-02 修正案 --
# commercial/docs/DESIGN-os-trust-chain-2026-09.md's 2026-09-02 拍板紀錄
# entry): root-B's GPT PARTUUID is now ALSO fixed at build time, the same
# way p2's --uuid= already fixes root-A's -- see
# classes/duduclaw-ab-partflags.bbclass's own comment on
# DUDUCLAW_AB_ROOTB_PARTUUID for the full Secure-Boot-compatibility
# reasoning (short version: patching a UKI's .cmdline bytes on the device,
# which is what a random root-B PARTUUID used to require, breaks the
# Authenticode signature Secure Boot checks over the whole PE image). This
# constant is what recipes-core/images/duduclaw-image-ab.bb's
# UKI_SLOTB_CMDLINE override bakes into the second, slot-B-targeted signed
# UKI (classes/duduclaw-ab-dualsign-uki.bbclass's do_uki_slotb task) — the
# same single-source-of-truth pairing p2's own comment already documents
# for root-A.
part --fstype=ext4 --label "root-b" --align 1024 --part-name="_empty" --part-type=4F68BCE3-E8CD-4DB1-96E7-FBCAF984B709 --fixed-size ${DUDUCLAW_AB_SLOT_SIZE_MB} --no-fstab-update --uuid="${DUDUCLAW_AB_ROOTB_PARTUUID}"

# root-b-verity (VER-V, 2026-09-02): root-B's own hash-tree twin, the
# same "reserved, empty until an update lands" role root-B itself has —
# root-B carries no real content at factory-build time (it is the `_empty`
# reserved slot above), so there is nothing to hash into a real tree yet
# either. Blank when DUDUCLAW_VERITY_ENABLE is unset, same mechanism as
# root-a-verity above (see that placeholder's own comment) — when set,
# classes/duduclaw-verity.bbclass fills this in as an EMPTY, `_empty`-named
# ext4 partition (no `--source` clause at all, matching root-B's own
# shape one line up), NOT a real hash tree. `_empty` is safe to reuse
# verbatim on this partition despite root-B ALSO using it: sysupdate's
# `[Target] MatchPartitionType=` (see the new
# recipes-duduclaw/duduclaw-ab-update/files/15-duduclaw-root-verity.transfer)
# filters by partition TYPE GUID first — root (SD_GPT_ROOT_X86_64) vs.
# root-verity (SD_GPT_ROOT_X86_64_VERITY) — so the two `_empty`-named
# partitions are never ambiguous to sysupdate even though they share a
# literal GPT Name string. (The task brief that seeded this class
# tentatively suggested a distinguishing name, "_verity_empty" — checked
# against this exact file's own root-B comment two blocks up, which
# already establishes, with citation, that `_empty` must be the LITERAL
# exact string upstream's own partition-target code matches on, not a
# freely-chosen pattern; `"_verity_empty"` would silently never match and
# leave this partition permanently un-writable by sysupdate. Corrected
# here rather than carried through uncritically.)
${DUDUCLAW_AB_ROOTB_VERITY_WKS_LINE}

# /data — update staging + gateway state, grows to fill whatever is left
# of the real disk (GPT attribute bit 59 / GrowFileSystem, same
# post-processing mechanism as root-B's NoAuto/ReadOnly — see the
# bbclass). Partition number 4 when DUDUCLAW_VERITY_ENABLE is unset
# (unchanged from before VER-V), 6 when it is set to "1" — see
# classes/duduclaw-ab-partflags.bbclass's own DUDUCLAW_AB_DATA_PARTNUM
# comment.
# Setting the bit alone does NOT make growth happen automatically on real
# hardware with a bigger disk than this factory image: that requires
# systemd-repart to actually run against a `repart.d/` drop-in on the
# target — wired up as of Y11-2 (2026-08-27) via
# recipes-duduclaw/duduclaw-firstboot/'s `duduclaw-firstboot-repart.sh` +
# `usr/lib/repart.d/30-data.conf`, now installed on this image too (see
# recipes-core/images/duduclaw-image-ab.bb's own IMAGE_INSTALL comment).
# PACKAGECONFIG[repart] itself (the systemd-repart binary being present at
# all) was already enabled layer-wide by Y8-1's
# recipes-core/systemd/systemd_%.bbappend. On THIS wks the size is simply
# fixed at DUDUCLAW_AB_DATA_SIZE_MB — sized for QEMU dev/test disks, not for
# a real mini-PC's full disk.
#
# GPT type is the generic `linux-generic` GUID (0FC63DAF-8483-4772-8E79-
# 3D69D8477DE4), matching the Debian line's own `30-data.conf` (`Type=
# linux-generic`) — /data is explicitly NOT a `root`-typed partition (it
# must never be a gpt-auto root candidate).
#
# --use-uuid + --fsoptions + --fspassno (Y11-2, 2026-08-27): THE FIX for the
# bug Y9-2 (same day, earlier round) reproduced live on this exact wks — with
# neither `--use-uuid` nor `--use-label` set, wic's own `update_fstab()`
# (plugins/imager/direct.py, `if part.use_uuid: ... UUID=... elif
# part.use_label: ... LABEL=... else: /dev/%s%s%d`) fell back to a raw
# `/dev/<disk><partnum>` device path, and wic's own internal disk-name
# default is `sda` — baked into /etc/fstab as `/dev/sda4` regardless of what
# the target machine's actual block device is. On this layer's QEMU machines
# the disk is virtio-blk (`/dev/vda*`), so `/dev/sda4` never existed: a cold
# boot hung ~90s on `A start job is running for /dev/sda4` before timing out
# and continuing without /data ever mounting — confirmed directly via
# `mount | grep /data` returning nothing on a fresh boot, not inferred. Every
# earlier round's `df -h /data` "looked mounted" because `df` silently
# reports the CONTAINING filesystem's usage when the mountpoint itself has
# nothing mounted on it — a fallback illusion, not a real PASS (see Y9-2's
# own "新發現" section in TODO-agent-first-os-2026-08.md for the full
# reproduction). This is the exact same fstab identifier gap
# files/wic/duduclaw-data-bootdisk.wks.in's own /data line already closed
# (Y9-1, same day) and real-QEMU-verified (`/dev/vda3 on /data type ext4
# (rw,relatime,x-systemd.growfs)`, reboot-idempotent, gateway self-config
# read back from it) — this line mirrors that ALREADY-PROVEN fix verbatim
# rather than reaching for a third, unverified identifier scheme: the
# Debian/mkosi appliance line's equivalent fstab entry
# (appliance/mkosi.extra/etc/fstab) uses `PARTLABEL=duduclaw-data`, but that
# is mkosi.repart's own `Label=` directive writing the GPT partition Name
# field directly — a DIFFERENT tool with a different fstab vocabulary. wic's
# kickstart parser has no `--use-partlabel` equivalent at all (confirmed by
# reading `ksparser.py`'s `part` argument list, same way Y9-2 confirmed
# `--label`/`--part-name` land on different GPT fields on the wic side); the
# two options wic actually offers are `--use-uuid` (ext4 filesystem UUID) and
# `--use-label` (ext4 filesystem LABEL via `mkfs -L` — NOT the raw GPT
# Name/PARTLABEL field p2/p3's own comments above already warn is a
# different, easily-confused namespace on this tool). Picking `--use-uuid`
# — the option with real QEMU boot evidence behind it on this exact layer —
# over blindly porting the Debian line's `PARTLABEL=` wording is a deliberate
# call to not repeat the class of mistake Y9-2 just finished fixing (Bug 1:
# assuming a Debian/mkosi vocabulary term maps onto the same-named wic
# option). `--fsoptions=defaults,x-systemd.growfs --fspassno=2` mirror the
# data wks's own /data line for the same reason stated there: a
# GPT-level GrowFileSystem resize alone does not resize the ext4 filesystem
# living inside the partition — `x-systemd.growfs` is the mount-time hook
# that does, and without it this line's own GrowFileSystem bit (set below by
# duduclaw-ab-partflags.bbclass) would silently do nothing once /data
# actually starts mounting.
part /data --fstype=ext4 --label duduclaw-data --align 1024 --use-uuid --uuid="${DUDUCLAW_AB_DATA_PARTUUID}" --fsoptions=defaults,x-systemd.growfs --fspassno=2 --part-name="duduclaw-data" --part-type=0FC63DAF-8483-4772-8E79-3D69D8477DE4 --size ${DUDUCLAW_AB_DATA_SIZE_MB}
